introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How can machines learn from experience without explicit programming?"
  overview: "This unit introduces the shift from <strong>Deterministic Programming</strong> (where we write the rules) to <strong>Machine Learning</strong> (where the computer finds the rules). You'll explore the taxonomy of AI, the specialized hardware that powers it, and the ethical dilemmas of algorithmic decision-making."
  journey: "You'll begin by defining <strong>Narrow AI</strong> vs <strong>General AI</strong>. Then, you'll categorize different types of learning (Supervised, Unsupervised, Reinforcement), examine the <strong>GPUs and TPUs</strong> that drive them, and finally debate the ethics of <strong>Bias</strong> and <strong>Accountability</strong>."

concepts:
  - title: "Taxonomy of Learning"
    icon: "fa-network-wired"
    content: |
      <p>Machine Learning isn't one thing. It's a family of techniques:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Supervised Learning:</strong> Training with labeled data (e.g., "This image is a cat").</li>
          <li><strong>Unsupervised Learning:</strong> Finding patterns in unlabeled data (e.g., "These customers buy similar things").</li>
          <li><strong>Reinforcement Learning:</strong> Learning through trial and error (reward/punishment).</li>
          <li><strong>Deep Learning:</strong> Using multi-layered Neural Networks.</li>
          <li><strong>Transfer Learning:</strong> Reusing a pre-trained model for a new task.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "AI Hardware"
    icon: "fa-microchip"
    content: |
      <p>AI requires massive parallel processing power. Traditional CPUs are too sequential.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>GPU (Graphics Processing Unit):</strong> Originally for games, now used for parallel AI math.</li>
          <li><strong>TPU (Tensor Processing Unit):</strong> Custom hardware designed by Google specifically for neural networks.</li>
          <li><strong>ASIC / Edge:</strong> Specialized chips running AI directly on devices (like phones) without the cloud.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Ethics of AI"
    icon: "fa-scale-balanced"
    content: |
      <p>With great power comes great responsibility. We must solve the "Black Box" problem:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Bias:</strong> If training data is prejudiced, the AI will be prejudiced.</li>
          <li><strong>Accountability:</strong> Who is responsible if an autonomous car crashes?</li>
          <li><strong>Transparency:</strong> Can we explain <em>why</em> the AI made a decision?</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Artificial Intelligence (AI)"
    definition: "Systems that perform tasks that normally require human intelligence (visual perception, speech recognition, decision-making)."
  - term: "Narrow AI"
    definition: "AI designed for a specific task (e.g., Chess, Spam filters). This is the only AI we have today."
  - term: "General AI (AGI)"
    definition: "Hypothetical AI that can understand any intellectual task that a human can. (Does not exist yet)."
  - term: "Machine Learning"
    definition: "A subset of AI where computers learn from data without being explicitly programmed for specific rules."
  - term: "Supervised Learning"
    definition: "Learning where the model is trained on a labeled dataset (Input-Output pairs)."
  - term: "Unsupervised Learning"
    definition: "Learning where the model looks for undetected patterns in a data set with no pre-existing labels."
  - term: "Reinforcement Learning"
    definition: "Learning by interacting with an environment; receiving positive rewards or negative penalties."
  - term: "Deep Learning"
    definition: "Machine learning based on artificial neural networks with multiple layers (hence 'deep')."
  - term: "GPU / TPU"
    definition: "Processors optimized for the parallel calculations required by neural networks."
  - term: "Algorithmic Bias"
    definition: "Systematic and repeatable errors in a computer system that create unfair outcomes, often due to biased training data."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "AI 'Understands'"
    correction: "Thinking AI has intent or consciousness. It is just a statistical engine finding patterns in numbers."
  - mistake: "General AI Exists"
    correction: "Believing 'Strong AI' (like in movies) is real. We strictly have Narrow AI today."
  - mistake: "Data is Neutral"
    correction: "The belief that data is objective. Human bias is encoded into every dataset we create."

